course: dictionary
lesson: 379
title: Any: one, some, or none
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Any is a small word with a big job. It can mean one or some, when it does not matter which one. «Any child can join» means every child, or whichever child you pick.
explain: In a question, any asks whether something is still there. «Is there any milk left?» wants to know whether some milk remains. «Not any» means none at all, so «not any milk» means zero milk.
explain: Any can also be an adverb meaning at all, as in «Is it any better?» Here it asks whether the thing has improved even a little. Look at the words around any to see which meaning fits, then try it in the sentence.
fact: Any means one or some, it does not matter which.
fact: «Not any» means none, zero.
fact: Any means every one, all: «Any child can join».
fact: Any (adverb) means at all: «Is it any better?»
fact: Phrases with any include «any more», «any time», «anyone», «anything» and «in any case».
exercise: «Any secret someone asks you to keep from your parents is a reason to tell them.» In this sentence, what does any mean? | None at all | Only a secret from a teacher | Very quickly | *Every one, all
exercise: «Is there any milk left in the fridge?» What is any asking about? | Whether the fridge is cold | Who drank it | Whether the milk is hot | *Whether some is still there
exercise: «Is it any better?» Here any is an adverb. What does it mean? | Very much better | Never ever | *At all | Only once
test: «If any of it lands on the ground, it is called a meteorite.» What does any mean here? | Every single piece in space | Only the biggest piece | None at all | *Some
test: Ash says «not any» means none. Is he right? | *Yes, not any means none, zero | No, it means very many | No, it means exactly one | No, it means all of them
exam: Which phrase with any means whatever happens? | *In any case | Not any | Any more | Any time
